3 — Build a labeled test dataset (covers + stegos)

You need covers (clean files) and corresponding stego files (same cover with hidden payload). Steps:

3.1 Collect clean covers

Take photos from multiple devices (phone, DSLR).

Download diverse images (various sizes, natural scenes, flat surfaces).

For PDFs/DOCs use real documents (invoices, reports) you own.

Organize:

dataset/
  covers/images/
  covers/pdfs/
  payloads/          # small payload files to hide (txt small, binary small)
  stego/images/
  stego/pdfs/

3.2 Choose stego generators (tools)

Use a mix of mature open-source tools to create diverse stego:

Steghide (image, audio) — commonly available.

OpenStego — image stego, payload embedding.

Outguess / stegseek — older but useful.

Custom scripts for LSB/append methods (Python or C++).

For PDFs/DOCX: use tools to embed attachments or create OLE objects (or script LibreOffice to embed a payload).

For video/audio: use ffmpeg to manipulate LSB / embed small payloads into audio channels (optional).

Note: If a tool isn’t available locally, implement simple LSB embedding in a small script (Python/Pillow) only for lab testcases.

3.3 Generate stego variants

For each cover, generate multiple stego variants:

Different payload sizes (e.g., 0.1%, 1%, 5% payload relative to cover capacity).

Different embedding algorithms.

Different image qualities/quantization levels (for JPEG).

With and without metadata.

Label each sample: cover_id, generator, payload_size, stego=true/false.

4 — Feature engineering & detection approaches

Since your detector is C++ and not ML-based (you said), plan for rule/statistics-based detection:

4.1 Useful features / checks (typical in stego detectors)

LSB / Bit-plane statistics: analyze least-significant bit histograms for irregularities.

Noise residuals: high-pass filter, measure residual correlations (SRM features).

JPEG quantization anomalies: DCT coefficient distribution checks, double-compression traces.

Image entropy / local entropy maps: unusually uniform or irregular patches.

Chi-square / RS analysis: statistical tests to find LSB changes.

Metadata presence: suspicious embedded payloads (attachments in PDF or OLE).

File-length anomalies: appended extra bytes after EOF.

Perceptual hash mismatch vs original (if you have original).

Implement a pipeline that computes a vector of feature scores and then uses rule thresholds to decide stego/not. For C++ you can implement these efficiently using libraries:

libjpeg / libpng for reading pixels / DCT coefficients.

OpenCV for filters/residuals.

Custom code for bit operations.

4.2 Decision logic

Single-threshold rules for specific detectors (e.g., RS test > T).

Combine multiple tests into a scoring function (weighted sum).

Optionally add a light ML classifier later using features (train offline in Python, port rules or thresholds to C++).

