6 — Evaluation: metrics, benchmark, thresholds

For each detector run over labeled dataset compute:

True Positive Rate (TPR) at several payload sizes.

False Positive Rate (FPR) on covers.

ROC curve / AUC (if using continuous score).

Precision/Recall and F1.

Latency: average detection time for file sizes (e.g., <10MB).

Set operational thresholds:

e.g., if score > 0.6 then block.
Tune to meet business targets (e.g., FPR ≤ 1%, TPR ≥ 80% for payload ≥1%).

7 — Integrate detector into Node.js upload pipeline
7.1 Flow in backend (summary)

User uploads file → Multer saves to tmp/ directory.

Immediately spawn your C++ detector CLI (child process) with filepath.

Parse returned JSON/exit code.

If stego=true → reject upload, remove tmp file, log event, notify sender.

Else → move file to permanent storage (S3/MinIO) and emit chat message.

7.2 Node.js example (spawn + parse)
const { spawn } = require('child_process');

function checkStego(filePath) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const proc = spawn('/opt/stegadetector/detect', [filePath]);

    let out = '';
    proc.stdout.on('data', (data) => out += data.toString());
    proc.stderr.on('data', (d) => console.error('stego err:', d.toString()));

    proc.on('close', (code) => {
      if (code === 2) return reject(new Error('stego tool error'));
      try {
        // tool outputs JSON
        const res = JSON.parse(out);
        resolve(res);
      } catch (e) {
        // fallback: use exit code
        resolve({ stego: code === 1, score: null });
      }
    });
  });
}

// Usage inside /upload handler
const res = await checkStego(tmpPath);
if (res.stego) {
  // block and notify
} else {
  // accept and forward
}

7.3 Performance considerations

Spawning a process per upload is OK for low volume. For higher throughput:

Create a pool of detector worker processes and send requests via IPC.

Or wrap detector as a long-running microservice (gRPC / REST) to avoid spawn overhead.

8 — Test harness & CI

Add unit tests in your repo: run dataset samples through detector on every push.

CI pipeline:

Build C++ detector on CI (GitHub Actions).

Run smoke tests on a small subset of dataset.

Fail builds if FPR > threshold.

9 — Monitoring, logging, admin controls

Log every blocked file: userId, fileHash, fileName, timestamp, detectorScore, detectorDetails.

Expose admin console to review quarantined files.

Allow admin to override (release file) if false positive.

Metric dashboards (Prometheus + Grafana) for:

Number of scans / minute

Blocked counts

Average latency

10 — Operational mitigations & UX

If suspicious, show helpful message to user: “File blocked — suspected hidden data. Contact admin for review.” Avoid technical jargon.

Quarantine flow: store blocked files in a secure quarantined bucket for admin review for a limited time (e.g., 7 days).

Fallback sanitation: For images flagged as suspicious, you can optionally attempt to re-encode (sharp) and if sanitized image is visually acceptable, allow with warning — but do this only after admin policy confirmation.

11 — Performance targets & scaling

Start targets:

Detection latency p50 < 1s for <=5MB; p95 < 5s for <=25MB.

Handle concurrent scans by scaling worker instances or process pool.

Scaling options:

Horizontal: run multiple detector worker services behind a queue (Rabbit/Kafka) and scale with Kubernetes.

Vertical: optimize C++ code, compile with optimizations, use SIMD for pixel ops.

12 — Example schedule (practical)

Week 1: Collect covers & generate stego dataset (LSB, appended, JPEG variants).

Week 2: Implement C++ detector core features (LSB & RS test + JPEG checks).

Week 3: Test detector, tune thresholds, build CLI, unit tests.

Week 4: Integrate with Node.js, build upload pipeline, add logging & quarantine.

Week 5: CI, admin UI for quarantined files, pilot with internal testers.

Week 6: Tune, document, prepare for scale.

13 — Acceptance criteria (concrete)

Detector blocks ≥ 80% of stego samples at payload ≥1% while keeping FPR ≤ 1% on cover set.

Average detection latency ≤ 3s for files ≤ 10MB on dev VM.

All blocked uploads are logged and quarantined.

Admin can view and release quarantined files.

